The same rules hold for limits of functions. If $f, g: X subset.eq CC -> CC$ have $lim_(z->a) f(z) = y$ and $lim_(z->a) g(z) = x$ at an accumulation point $a$ of $X$, then

$
  lim_(z->a) (f(z) + g(z)) = y + x, quad lim_(z->a) (f(z) g(z)) = y x, quad lim_(z->a) (f(z)/g(z)) = y/x,
$

the quotient requiring $g(z) != 0$ for all $z in X$ and $x != 0$.
